-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 2. HAPUTALE
-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

CALL ict_insert_dest_if_missing(
    'Haputale',
    'haputale',
    'A cool, misty hill-town offering Lipton\'s Seat panorama, working tea estates, and a quieter, more authentic alternative to Nuwara Eliya.',
    'Haputale sits at 1,431 metres on the edge of the southern escarpment — a small, unassuming hill-town that commands one of the most breathtaking views in Sri Lanka. On a clear day from Lipton\'s Seat, you can see both the southern and eastern coasts. The town itself is functional rather than beautiful — a single main road, a bus stand, and a railway station. But the landscape around it is magnificent. The tea estates roll over the hills like a green sea. The Dambatenne Tea Factory, built by Sir Thomas Lipton himself, still operates. The train from Haputale to Ella is one of the most scenic segments in the country. Haputale is quieter than Nuwara Eliya, less touristy than Ella, and cooler than both. It rewards the traveller who stops here instead of rushing through.',
    'Lipton\'s Seat is the main draw — a viewpoint named after the tea magnate who used to sit here to survey his empire. The drive or hike up is beautiful. The view at the top is, on a clear day, one of the best in Sri Lanka. The Dambatenne Tea Factory tour is proper — you see the full process from withering to grading, and the tasting room has the best cup of tea you will have in the country. Adisham Bungalow is a charming Tudor-style monastery built by a British planter. And the train journey from Haputale south toward Ella snakes along the edge of the escarpment, with views that drop into tea valleys hundreds of metres below.',
    'Hike or drive to Lipton\'s Seat for sunrise (5 AM start, 7 km from the main gate). Tour the Dambatenne Tea Factory — the tasting room alone is worth it. Visit Adisham Bungalow (a tranquil colonial monastery and gardens). Walk through the tea estates on the edge of town. Take the train from Haputale to Ella (1.5 hours, possibly the most scenic stretch). Visit the Haputale market if you arrive on a market day. Photograph the sunset from the Andagala viewpoint. Eat a traditional Sri Lankan rice and curry at a local guesthouse.',
    'January to April is the best time for clear views from Lipton\'s Seat. The rest of the year is misty and often wet. Mornings are consistently clearer than afternoons. If you only have one shot, take it in the early morning.',
    'About 170 kilometres from Colombo — 4 hours by car via the A4 highway through Balangoda. The road climbs steeply near Beragala. Trains from Colombo to Haputale run daily (6-7 hours, beautiful journey). From Ella, the train is 1.5 hours or the road is 2 hours via Passara Road.',
    'Two nights is comfortable. Day one: arrive, afternoon tea factory tour. Day two: pre-dawn Lipton\'s Seat, return for breakfast, Adisham Bungalow in the afternoon. Day three: morning train to Ella or onward journey. The pacing here is slow by design.',
    'Accommodation is limited compared to Nuwara Eliya or Ella, but the quality is improving. Sunny Side Guesthouse offers friendly service and good food. The Cozy Cottage is charming. Haputale Inn is a reliable mid-range option. For a special stay, Thotalagala is a restored plantation bungalow with old-world elegance.',
    'Lipton\'s Seat access opens at 6 AM — arrive early for the best light and to beat the tourist vans from Nuwara Eliya. The road up is steep and winding; a private car is much better than the bus. The tea factory tour is free and they do not pressure you to buy. The train station has no advance reservation system — buy your ticket at least 30 minutes before departure.',
    '[{"q":"Can I walk up to Lipton\'s Seat?","a":"Yes, it is a 7 km hike from the main gate. Allow 1.5 hours. The walk through the tea estates is beautiful."},{"q":"Is Haputale better than Nuwara Eliya?","a":"They are different. Haputale is quieter and more authentic. Nuwara Eliya has more accommodation and dining options."},{"q":"Do I need warm clothes?","a":"Yes. The temperature drops to 10-15°C at night. A fleece is essential in the early morning."}]',
    'Plan Your Haputale Tea Country Experience',
    'We can arrange a private driver and guide for Lipton\'s Seat sunrise, tea factory tours, and hill-country transfers.',
    'Uva Province',
    '15–25°C',
    'Tea & Viewpoints',
    '1–2 Nights',
    '1–2 nights',
    'hill',
    'Hill Country',
    '🍵',
    114, 208, 5.5, 'H',
    'Haputale Travel Guide for Sri Lanka Tea Country | Island Culture Travels',
    'Discover Haputale: Lipton\'s Seat views, tea factory tours, Adisham Bungalow, and less-touristed hill-country charm. Private transfers from Colombo or Ella.',
    51, 1, 1, 'Tea & Viewpoints',
    'Panoramic views from Lipton\'s Seat and authentic tea-country life without the crowds',
    'Nuwara Eliya, Ella, Horton Plains, Kandy',
    'Nanu Oya: 30 min. Ella: 1.5 hrs (train). Nuwara Eliya: 1.5 hrs. Horton Plains: 1.5 hrs. Kandy: 4 hrs. Colombo: 4 hrs.',
    '{"months":[{"month":"Jan","rating":"great"},{"month":"Feb","rating":"great"},{"month":"Mar","rating":"great"},{"month":"Apr","rating":"great"},{"month":"May","rating":"avoid"},{"month":"Jun","rating":"avoid"},{"month":"Jul","rating":"avoid"},{"month":"Aug","rating":"okay"},{"month":"Sep","rating":"okay"},{"month":"Oct","rating":"avoid"},{"month":"Nov","rating":"avoid"},{"month":"Dec","rating":"okay"}],"note":"Jan-Apr for clearest views from Lipton\'s Seat. Mornings are consistently better than afternoons."}',
    'Jan–Apr',
    'Aug–Sep',
    'May–Jul, Oct–Nov',
    'Cool and misty. Much quieter and more authentic than Nuwara Eliya.',
    'Jan',
    'Apr',
    '{"nationalities":["UK","GER"],"top_activities":["Lipton\'s Seat hike","Tea factory tours","Scenic train rides","Adisham Bungalow visit"],"traveler_types":["couple","solo","nature","photographer"]}',
    '[{"platform":"TripAdvisor","score":"4.5","url":"","notes":"Top-rated hill-country viewpoint"},{"platform":"Booking.com","score":"8.5","url":"","notes":"Limited but good accommodation"}]',
    'Easy',
    'Car, Train',
    'Quiet',
    'Logistical Effort: Off the main tourist trail. Transport is limited; a private driver is essential. The train is scenic but slow. Lipton\'s Seat access road is steep and winding.',
    '[{"name":"Lipton\'s Seat","type":"viewpoint","distance":"7 km"},{"name":"Adisham Bungalow","type":"heritage","distance":"3 km"},{"name":"Dambatenne Tea Factory","type":"experience","distance":"5 km"}]',
    '[{"slug":"nuwara-eliya","why":"Continue the tea-country route north through working estates"},{"slug":"ella","why":"Connect to Ella via the most scenic train segment in Sri Lanka"},{"slug":"horton-plains","why":"Combine highland plateau walk with tea-country drives"}]'
);

-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 3. WILPATTU NATIONAL PARK
-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

CALL ict_insert_dest_if_missing(
    'Wilpattu National Park',
    'wilpattu',
    'Sri Lanka\'s largest national park — a wild, uncrowded landscape of sand-rimmed lakes with the best leopard sightings in the country.',
    'Wilpattu is Sri Lanka\'s largest national park at 1,317 square kilometres. It is also the least commercialized. While Yala gets the crowds, Wilpattu offers a raw, untamed safari experience. The landscape is dominated by natural sand-rimmed lakes called "willus" — depressions that fill with rainwater and attract wildlife throughout the dry season. The park reopened fully after the civil war and the wildlife has thrived. Leopard sightings here are frequent and the experience is private — you often have an entire willu to yourself. Sloth bears are common. The birdlife is excellent. It is not as accessible as Yala, which is why it remains uncrowded. For seasoned safari-goers, that is exactly the point.',
    'The leopards are the headline. Wilpattu has a high density of these elusive cats and the terrain — open forest around the willus — makes them easier to spot than in Yala\'s dense jungle. The sloth bear population is among the best in Sri Lanka. The willus themselves are beautiful — mirrors of water reflecting the surrounding forest. The park\'s remoteness means you can sit at a willu for an hour without seeing another vehicle. The coastal section of the park is wild and rarely visited. And at the end of a safari, the sunsets over the scrubland are spectacular.',
    'Morning and afternoon jeep safaris (start at 6 AM or 2 PM). Leopard tracking at the willu lakes. Sloth bear watching (particularly in the morning). Birding near the coastal section. Visit the Kudiramalai Point on the coast. Photograph the willu reflections at golden hour. Drive the coastal road from the park to Kalpitiya for a different landscape. Visit the ancient hermitages scattered near the park boundary.',
    'February to October is the main season. The park closes during the north-east monsoon. May and June are peak for leopard sightings as the willus shrink. November and December bring the monsoon — many trails close. January is shoulder season with moderate conditions.',
    'About 200 kilometres from Colombo — 4 hours by car via the A12 highway through Puttalam. The road to the park entrance from the highway is unpaved and slow. Buses run from Colombo to Puttalam, from where you need private transport. Fly to Colombo and drive; there is no airport nearby.',
    'Two nights is the minimum. Day one: arrive by midday, afternoon safari. Day two: early-morning safari, rest, afternoon safari. Day three: morning safari and depart. The park repays time spent sitting quietly at the willus.',
    'Accommodation is mostly outside the park. Big Game Camp is the best — tented luxury near the park boundary. Leopard Trails has a premium tented camp inside the park area. Mahoora Tent Camp is a good budget option. Kalpitiya beach hotels are 1 hour away.',
    'Book jeeps and accommodation in advance for the peak months. The park is strict about entry times — no entry after 4 PM. The willus are best in the late afternoon when the light is golden. Pack insect repellent and sunblock. The sandflies near the coastal section are aggressive. Carry binoculars for leopard spotting.',
    '[{"q":"Is Wilpattu better than Yala?","a":"For crowds: yes. For raw, uncrowded wilderness: yes. For guaranteed leopards: neither can guarantee, but Wilpattu has comparable densities."},{"q":"How many days do I need?","a":"Two nights and three safaris is ideal. One night is tight."},{"q":"Does the park close during monsoon?","a":"Parts of the park close during the NE monsoon (Nov-Dec). Check current conditions before booking."}]',
    'Plan Your Wilpattu Wilderness Safari',
    'We arrange private jeeps and premium tented camps at Wilpattu — the uncrowded alternative to Yala for serious safari lovers.',
    'North Western Province',
    '27–34°C',
    'Wildlife Safari',
    '1–2 Days',
    '1–2 days',
    'wildlife',
    'Wildlife',
    '🐆',
    76, 52, 7.0, 'W',
    'Wilpattu National Park Safari Guide | Island Culture Travels',
    'Plan a Wilpattu safari: Sri Lanka\'s largest and least commercialised national park. Leopards, sloth bears, willu lakes, and private jeep experiences.',
    52, 1, 1, 'Wildlife Safari',
    'Sri Lanka\'s most uncrowded leopard and sloth bear safari destination',
    'Anuradhapura, Jaffna, Kalpitiya, Mannar',
    'Anuradhapura: 1.5 hrs. Puttalam: 1 hr. Kalpitiya: 1.5 hrs. Jaffna: 3 hrs. Colombo: 4 hrs.',
    '{"months":[{"month":"Jan","rating":"okay"},{"month":"Feb","rating":"great"},{"month":"Mar","rating":"great"},{"month":"Apr","rating":"great"},{"month":"May","rating":"great"},{"month":"Jun","rating":"great"},{"month":"Jul","rating":"great"},{"month":"Aug","rating":"great"},{"month":"Sep","rating":"great"},{"month":"Oct","rating":"great"},{"month":"Nov","rating":"avoid"},{"month":"Dec","rating":"avoid"}],"note":"Peak leopard sightings May-Jun. Park closes in Dec during NE monsoon."}',
    'Feb–Oct',
    'Jan, Nov',
    'Dec (partial park closure)',
    'Dry and sunny. Unique sand-rimmed lakes (willus) attract wildlife.',
    'Feb',
    'Oct',
    '{"nationalities":["UK","GER","AUS"],"top_activities":["Leopard tracking","Sloth bear sightings","Birding","Private picnics at willus"],"traveler_types":["nature","photographer","safari","adventure"]}',
    '[{"platform":"TripAdvisor","score":"4.5","url":"","notes":"Best uncrowded safari in Sri Lanka"},{"platform":"GetYourGuide","score":"4.9","url":"","notes":"Top-rated wildlife experience"}]',
    'Easy (safari)',
    'Car',
    'Quiet',
    'Seasonality: Closes during the NE monsoon (Nov-Dec). Logistical Effort: Less crowded than Yala, but fewer luxury tented camps available inside the park. Infrastructure is more basic. Strict park entry times are enforced.',
    '[{"name":"Kudiramalai Point","type":"coast","distance":"park boundary"},{"name":"Kalpitiya","slug":"kalpitiya","type":"beach","distance":"1.5 hrs"},{"name":"Anuradhapura","slug":"anuradhapura","type":"heritage","distance":"1.5 hrs"}]',
    '[{"slug":"anuradhapura","why":"Combine ancient city heritage with Wilpattu wilderness on a northern route"},{"slug":"kalpitiya","why":"Pair safari with kitesurfing or dolphin watching"},{"slug":"mannar","why":"Extended northern loop for remote coast and birding"}]'
);

-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 4. KALPITIYA
-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

CALL ict_insert_dest_if_missing(
    'Kalpitiya',
    'kalpitiya',
    'A narrow peninsula with world-class kitesurfing lagoons, wild dolphin pods of 1,000+, and untouched white sandbars.',
    'Kalpitiya sits on the north-west coast, a 30-kilometre peninsula jutting into the Indian Ocean. It is not a typical beach resort — it is raw, windy, and wild. The lagoon system on the eastern side creates perfect flat-water conditions for kitesurfing. The Bar Reef Marine Sanctuary off the west coast is one of the most biodiverse coral reefs in Sri Lanka. The dolphin population in the gulf is staggering — pods of 1,000 spinner dolphins are common. The beaches are empty. The sunsets are epic. Kalpitiya attracts two crowds: die-hard kitesurfers who come for the wind, and travellers seeking the remote, untouched coast. There is very little luxury here; the appeal is the elemental experience.',
    'The kitesurfing is the biggest draw — the lagoon conditions are among the best in Asia. The dolphin-watching is extraordinary: pods of spinner dolphins perform aerial displays in the gulf. The Bar Reef snorkelling is excellent when conditions are right. The sandbars at low tide create ephemeral islands you can walk to. And the Dutch Bay area has a peaceful, end-of-the-road atmosphere that is rare in Sri Lanka.',
    'Kitesurf or take lessons at the Kalpitiya Lagoon (May-Sep is peak wind season). Go dolphin-watching in the gulf (Oct-Feb for calm seas). Snorkel at Bar Reef Marine Sanctuary. Walk the sandbars at low tide. Visit the Dutch fort at Kalpitiya town. Explore the fishing villages along the peninsula. Drive to the tip for sunset. Sandboard on the dunes near the lagoon mouth.',
    'May to September brings the south-west monsoon and the best kitesurfing wind. October to February is calm water and peak dolphin season. March, April, and October are transitional months with less reliable conditions. The annual wind pattern is strong and predictable.',
    'About 150 kilometres from Colombo — 3 hours by car via the A3 highway through Negombo and Chilaw. The road is good until you turn off at Puttalam. Public transport is limited; a private car is essential. The nearest airport is Colombo (BIA), 2.5 hours by car.',
    'Three to five days is common for kitesurfers. Three days minimum: two full days on the water and one for dolphin watching. Non-kiters can cover the highlights in two days but the relaxed pace makes a longer stay appealing.',
    'Accommodation is basic but improving. Kalpitiya Beach Resort is the most comfortable. Alankuda Beach Resort is popular with kitesurfers. For budget, several small guesthouses operate near the lagoon. The kitesurf camps like Kite-Surf-LK offer dorm and package stays.',
    'The wind season (May-Sep) is strict for kitesurfing — outside these months the conditions are unreliable. Dolphin trips should be booked with responsible operators who do not chase the pods. The mosquitoes at dusk are fierce. Bring sunblock, a rash guard, and DEET. The reef access is tide-dependent.',
    '[{"q":"Do I need to know kitesurfing to visit?","a":"No. Non-kiters can enjoy dolphin watching, sandbars, and the laid-back atmosphere. Lessons are available for beginners."},{"q":"When is the best wind for kitesurfing?","a":"May to September. The wind is strongest and most consistent in June and July."},{"q":"Is Kalpitiya crowded?","a":"No. It is one of the least crowded coastal destinations in Sri Lanka."}]',
    'Plan Your Kalpitiya Kitesurfing & Dolphin Trip',
