-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 5. GAL OYA NATIONAL PARK
-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

CALL ict_insert_dest_if_missing(
    'Gal Oya National Park',
    'gal-oya',
    'Sri Lanka\'s only park where you can do walking safaris with indigenous Vedda trackers and boat safaris on a vast reservoir.',
    'Gal Oya National Park covers 25,900 hectares in the eastern dry zone, centred on the ancient Senanayaka Samudra reservoir. It is remote, uncrowded, and offers something no other park in Sri Lanka provides: walking safaris guided by Vedda forest trackers. The Vedda are Sri Lanka\'s indigenous people, and their knowledge of the forest — tracking animals, identifying plants, reading the weather — is extraordinary. On a walking safari, you leave the jeep behind and follow them on foot. The boat safari on the reservoir is equally special — elephants swim between islands, birds roost in dead trees, and the reflections of the forest in the still water create a landscape of extraordinary beauty.',
    'The walking safaris are the unique draw. No other park in Sri Lanka allows walking safaris (outside a few controlled areas). The Vedda guides bring a depth of knowledge that transforms a safari into a cultural experience. The boat safaris on the reservoir are spectacular — elephants swimming between islands is a regular sight. The birdlife is excellent: painted storks, lesser adjutants, and the endemic Sri Lankan junglefowl. The park\'s isolation means you will see few other vehicles.',
    'Walking safari with Vedda trackers (must be booked in advance). Boat safari on the Senanayaka Samudra reservoir. Morning and afternoon jeep safaris. Birdwatching at the reservoir edge. Visit the Vedda village near the park boundary. Photograph elephants swimming between islands. Explore the Inginiyagala area for temple ruins. Camping near the park for a full wilderness experience.',
    'March to July is the best period — the dry season concentrates animals around the reservoir. August to February brings more rain and animals disperse. The walking safaris are best from April to July when the forest floor is driest.',
    'About 340 kilometres from Colombo — 6 hours by car via the A4 highway through Badulla and then down to the east. The road from Colombo to Gal Oya is long. The nearest town is Ampara (30 km from the park). Private transport is essential. There is no public transport to the park.',
    'Two or three nights is ideal. Day one: arrive, afternoon boat safari. Day two: early-morning walking safari, afternoon jeep safari. Day three: morning boat safari and depart. The walking safari is a full-morning commitment.',
    'Gal Oya Lodge is the only upscale property — eight villas overlooking the forest with a pool and excellent guiding. Alternatively, simple guesthouses in Inginiyagala or Ampara. Gal Oya Lodge is strongly recommended for the full experience.',
    'The walking safari is physically moderate — you walk through forest, not on trails, for about 3 hours. Wear sturdy shoes and long trousers. The Vedda guides speak basic English but a translator can be arranged. Book the walking safari well in advance as the guides are limited. The boat safari is most rewarding in the late afternoon.',
    '[{"q":"Is the walking safari safe?","a":"Yes. The Vedda trackers are expert. You are accompanied by an armed ranger for safety."},{"q":"Do I need special fitness for the walking safari?","a":"Moderate fitness is recommended. The walk is about 3 km over forest terrain."},{"q":"Can I see elephants from the boat?","a":"Yes. Elephants swim between the reservoir islands and are frequently seen from the water."}]',
    'Plan Your Gal Oya Walking Safari Experience',
    'We can arrange private guided walking safaris with Vedda trackers, boat safaris, and premium lodge stays at Gal Oya.',
    'Uva Province',
    '26–33°C',
