-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 6. RITIGALA
-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

CALL ict_insert_dest_if_missing(
    'Ritigala',
    'ritigala',
    'A mystical Strict Nature Reserve where ancient monastery ruins lie hidden beneath a canopy of silence — picking a single leaf is forbidden here.',
    'Ritigala is unlike any other archaeological site in Sri Lanka. It is a Strict Nature Reserve, the highest level of protection in the country. The ancient monastery complex — dating back to the 1st century BC — is overgrown by forest, with stone bridges, meditation platforms, and monastic cells swallowed by the jungle. The silence is the first thing you notice. There are no sounds of traffic, no tourists, no vendors. Just birdsong and the rustle of leaves. The forest itself is sacred. You cannot pick a leaf. You cannot smoke. You must hike with a mandatory guide. Ritigala is not a place to tick off a list. It is a place to experience — a spiritual, archaeological, and ecological wonder in one.',
    'The stone bridges are the iconic feature — massive granite slabs laid across ravines in a construction style that is still not fully understood. The meditation platforms offer views through the trees to the plains below. The lotus ponds are exquisitely carved. The sense of solitude is profound. The butterfly diversity is among the highest in Sri Lanka. And the hike itself — through the forest, past the overgrown ruins — feels like exploring a lost civilisation.',
    'Hike the main trail to the monastery complex (2 hours, moderate). Walk across the ancient stone bridges. Explore the meditation platforms and monastic cells. Spot endemic butterflies and birds. Photograph the overgrown ruins through the forest canopy. Visit the medicinal plant trail near the base. Enjoy the complete silence at the meditation platforms. Visit the nearby Yapahuwa rock fortress on the same day.',
    'February to September is the dry season and the best time to visit. October to January brings rain and slippery trails. The site is at its best in the early morning before the heat builds. Weekdays are much quieter than weekends.',
    'About 170 kilometres from Colombo — 3.5 hours by car via the A1 highway to Dambulla, then north on the A9. The turn-off is between Dambulla and Anuradhapura, near Maradankadawala. The road to the reserve is unpaved for the last 3 km. Private transport is essential.',
    'Half a day at the site is sufficient. Most visitors combine it with Yapahuwa or Anuradhapura on the same day. If you are staying nearby, the hotels in Dambulla or Habarana are 45 minutes away.',
    'There is no accommodation near Ritigala. Stay in Dambulla (45 min), Habarana (45 min), or Anuradhapura (1.5 hrs). The hotels in Habarana are the most convenient for a combined Ritigala-Yapahuwa day trip.',
    'A guide is mandatory — arrange at the reserve office. The walk involves climbing stairs cut into the rock and navigating uneven terrain. Wear sturdy shoes. Take water — there are no facilities inside the reserve. The rules are strict: no littering, no smoking, no taking anything from the forest. The experience is worth the effort.',
    '[{"q":"Why can\'t I pick leaves?","a":"Ritigala is a Strict Nature Reserve. Everything — living and dead — is protected. You cannot remove anything."},{"q":"Can I visit Ritigala and Yapahuwa on the same day?","a":"Yes. They are in the same region and together make an excellent day trip from Dambulla or Habarana."},{"q":"Is the hike difficult?","a":"Moderate. The path involves uneven stone steps and slopes. Allow 2 hours for the round trip."}]',
    'Plan Your Ritigala Mystical Ruins Experience',
