-- 7. USSANGODA
-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

CALL ict_insert_dest_if_missing(
    'Ussangoda',
    'ussangoda',
    'A surreal, Mars-like red-earth plateau on the south coast steeped in Ramayana mythology and overlooking the deep blue ocean.',
    'Ussangoda is one of the most unusual landscapes in Sri Lanka. It is a red-earth plateau spanning several hectares, almost completely devoid of vegetation, sitting directly above the Indian Ocean. The soil is striking — a deep, rusty red that contrasts with the blue sea and green scrub of the surrounding area. According to Ramayana mythology, this is the site where King Ravana\'sPushpaka Vimana (flying chariot) landed. The geological reality is no less interesting: the soil contains high levels of iron and other minerals that prevent most plant growth. The result is a landscape that feels alien, Martian, and entirely unexpected in lush Sri Lanka.',
    'The landscape itself is the attraction. The red earth, the stark contrast with the ocean, and the complete absence of vegetation create extraordinary photo opportunities. The mythology adds depth — local stories of the Ramayana connect this place to events over 5,000 years old. The coast below the plateau is rugged and beautiful. The sunsets are spectacular. And the lack of tourist infrastructure means you will likely have the place to yourself.',
    'Walk across the red-earth plateau (allow 1 hour). Photograph the surreal landscape at golden hour. Visit the small temple at the entrance dedicated to the Ramayana connection. Explore the coast below the plateau (rough access). Visit the nearby Dondra Head Lighthouse (the tallest in Sri Lanka). Combine with nearby Tangalle or Bundala for a full day on the deep south coast.',
    'December to March is the best time — dry and clear. The rest of the year brings rain that makes the red earth darker and less striking. Visit in the late afternoon (4 PM) for the best light. Midday is extremely hot with no shade.',
    'About 170 kilometres from Colombo — 2.5 hours by car on the Southern Expressway to Matara, then along the A2 coastal road. The turn-off to Ussangoda is between Dondra and Hambantota. A 4WD is recommended for the last section of unpaved road to the plateau top.',
    'A one-hour stop is enough for the main plateau. It works well as a photo stop between Galle/Tangalle and Yala. No overnight stay is necessary.',
    'There is no accommodation on site. Stay in Tangalle (30 min) or Hambantota (30 min). Tangalle has the best beach hotels in the region.',
    'The plateau has no shade — bring a hat, sunblock, and water. The road to the top is rough and a 4WD is useful. The site is free and open access. The best photos are in the late afternoon when the low sun warms the red tones. Respect the temple at the entrance — it is an active religious site.',
    '[{"q":"Is Ussangoda accessible without a 4WD?","a":"The last section is rough. A standard car can manage in dry conditions but 4WD is recommended."},{"q":"What is the significance of the red soil?","a":"Geologically, it is iron-rich laterite soil. Mythologically, Ramayana traditions associate it with Ravana."},{"q":"How long should I spend here?","a":"30 minutes to 1 hour is enough for photos and exploration."}]',
    'Plan Your Ussangoda Experience',
    'We can arrange a private car with 4WD access to Ussangoda\'s red-earth plateau — a unique photo stop on the deep south coast.',
