-- 8. YAPAHUWA
-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

CALL ict_insert_dest_if_missing(
    'Yapahuwa',
    'yapahuwa',
    'A spectacular rock fortress with an ornate 13th-century staircase — the Anti-Sigiriya with a fraction of the tourists.',
    'Yapahuwa served as the capital of Sri Lanka in the late 13th century for a brief 23-year period. The rock rises 90 metres above the surrounding plains, crowned with the remains of a royal palace and a Buddhist temple. What makes Yapahuwa special is the staircase. Carved into the rock, it ascends through a series of ornate arches and landings, flanked by stone carvings that are among the finest in Sri Lanka. The site is dramatically under-visited. On an average day, you might see a handful of other tourists. The climb is steep but manageable, and the view from the top — rice paddies, coconut palms, and distant hills — is broad and beautiful.',
    'The ornamental staircase is the highlight. The carved stone arches, the lion and dancer figures, and the massive granite blocks fitted into the rock face are exquisite. The palace ruins at the top are sparse but the terrace has atmosphere. The rock itself is dramatic — an isolated granite monolith rising from the flat plains. The silence on top is absolute. And the lack of crowds means you can take your time, sit on the summit, and absorb the history without hurry.',
    'Climb the ornamental staircase (the main attraction — go slowly and examine the carvings). Explore the palace ruins on the summit. Visit the small temple at the base (active — dress respectfully). Walk the base of the rock to see the defensive ditches and ramparts. Climb the smaller rock adjacent to Yapahuwa for a different viewpoint. Visit the nearby hakgala archaeological site. Combine with Ritigala for a full day of heritage exploration.',
    'December to April is the dry season and the best time to climb. May to July brings heat and occasional rain. The early morning (7-9 AM) is the best time to avoid the heat. The site is open year-round.',
    'About 140 kilometres from Colombo — 3 hours by car via the A1 highway to Kurunegala, then north. Yapahuwa is located between Kurunegala and Anuradhapura on the A10 highway. The turn-off is well-signed. Private transport is recommended; buses stop on the main road, 1 km from the site.',
    'Half a day is sufficient for Yapahuwa itself. It works well as a stop between Colombo and Anuradhapura or as a combined day with Ritigala. No overnight stay is needed at the site.',
    'Stay in Kurunegala (45 min), Dambulla (1 hr), or Anuradhapura (1 hr). The hotels on the Negombo-Anuradhapura route are convenient for an overnight stop.',
    'The climb is steep — about 500 steps cut into the rock. Wear sturdy shoes. Take water. The carvings on the staircase are fragile — do not touch or lean on them. The ticket office at the base sells entry only; no guides are necessary. The site has very few facilities — bring everything you need.',
    '[{"q":"Why is Yapahuwa called the Anti-Sigiriya?","a":"It offers a similar rock fortress experience — climb, summit ruins, panoramic views — but with a fraction of the tourists."},{"q":"How does the climb compare to Sigiriya?","a":"Yapahuwa is shorter (90m vs 200m) and quicker (15-20 min vs 45-60 min). The staircase is the main attraction."},{"q":"Can I visit on the same day as Ritigala?","a":"Yes. Yapahuwa and Ritigala are in the same region and can be combined for a full heritage day."}]',
    'Plan Your Yapahuwa Rock Fortress Visit',
    'We can route your Sri Lanka itinerary through Yapahuwa — the uncrowded rock fortress with the finest medieval staircase on the island.',
    'North Western Province',
