-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 11. BUNDALA NATIONAL PARK
-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

CALL ict_insert_dest_if_missing(
    'Bundala National Park',
    'bundala',
    'A Ramsar wetland paradise for birders — shallow lagoons, golden sand dunes, and thousands of greater flamingos in winter.',
    'Bundala National Park covers 6,216 hectares of shallow lagoons, scrub jungle, and sand dunes on Sri Lanka\'s deep south coast. It is a Ramsar wetland of international importance, a critical stopover for migratory waterbirds along the Central Asian Flyway. During the northern winter (November to March), the lagoons fill with tens of thousands of birds: greater flamingos, pelicans, painted storks, openbills, and countless species of waders and terns. The landscape is open and golden — the dunes, the dry scrub, and the lagoons reflecting the sky create a peaceful, light-filled environment. Bundala is quieter than Yala, more accessible than Kumana, and arguably the best birding park in Sri Lanka.',
    'The flamingos are the headline — flocks of hundreds in the deeper lagoons, pink against the blue water. The lagoon system is excellent for photography, especially in the morning and late afternoon. The shallow water makes bird-watching easy. The leopard population in Bundala is small but present. Crocodiles sunbathe on the mudbanks. The turtles nest on the beach section of the park. It is not a "big animal" park — this is a birders\' paradise, and visitors who love birds will find it extraordinary.',
    'Birdwatching drives through the lagoon system (2-3 hours). Flamingo photography — the main lagoon in the morning. Crocodile spotting at the water\'s edge. Beach walk to the turtle nesting area. Visit the park\'s watchtower for panoramic views. Photograph the sand dunes at golden hour. Extend to the nearby Kalamatiya bird sanctuary. Combine with a visit to Yala (1 hour away) or Tangalle.',
    'November to March is the peak season — European migratory birds arrive in huge numbers. April and October are shoulder months with fewer migrants. May to September is the off-season — fewer birds but the resident population is still interesting. The flamingos are most reliable from December to February.',
    'About 195 kilometres from Colombo — 2.5 hours by car on the Southern Expressway to Hambantota, then a short drive east. The park entrance is on the A2 coastal highway between Hambantota and Yala. From Tangalle, it is 45 minutes east. From Yala, it is 30 minutes west.',
    'Half a day (2-3 hours) is enough for a birding drive. It works perfectly as a stop between Yala and Tangalle or as a morning add-on to a Yala itinerary. No overnight stay is required.',
    'Stay in Tangalle (45 min) or near Yala (30 min). Tangalle offers the best south coast beach hotels. Near Yala, the safari lodges are comfortable. There is no accommodation inside the park.',
    'The best birding is in the early morning (6-9 AM). The afternoon heat makes birds less active. Bring binoculars — they are essential. The mosquitoes near the lagoons can be intense. The jeep roads are sandy in places. The park has two entry gates; the main gate is better for birds.',
    '[{"q":"Is Bundala better than Yala for birding?","a":"Yes — Bundala is primarily a bird sanctuary. Yala is better for mammals. Serious birders should visit both."},{"q":"When are the flamingos in Bundala?","a":"From November to March, peaking in December and January."},{"q":"Can I visit Bundala and Yala on the same day?","a":"Yes. They are 30 minutes apart. An early morning at Bundala and an afternoon at Yala works well."}]',
    'Plan Your Bundala Birding Safari',
    'We can arrange a private birding safari at Bundala with a knowledgeable guide who knows every lagoon and every species.',
    'Southern Province',
    '27–33°C',
    'Birding / Ramsar',
    'Half day',
    'Half day',
    'wildlife',
    'Wildlife',
    '🦩',
    135, 280, 5.5, 'Bu',
    'Bundala National Park Birding Guide | Island Culture Travels',
    'Plan a Bundala birding safari: Ramsar wetlands with greater flamingos, pelicans, storks, and hundreds of migratory bird species — near Yala and Tangalle.',
    60, 1, 1, 'Birding / Ramsar',
    'A Ramsar wetland paradise with thousands of greater flamingos and migratory birds',
    'Yala, Tangalle, Hambantota, Tissamaharama',
    'Yala: 30 min. Tangalle: 45 min. Hambantota: 20 min. Colombo: 2.5 hrs.',
    '{"months":[{"month":"Jan","rating":"great"},{"month":"Feb","rating":"great"},{"month":"Mar","rating":"great"},{"month":"Apr","rating":"okay"},{"month":"May","rating":"avoid"},{"month":"Jun","rating":"avoid"},{"month":"Jul","rating":"avoid"},{"month":"Aug","rating":"avoid"},{"month":"Sep","rating":"avoid"},{"month":"Oct","rating":"okay"},{"month":"Nov","rating":"great"},{"month":"Dec","rating":"great"}],"note":"Nov-Mar for migratory birds. Dec-Feb for peak flamingo numbers."}',
